783203125, -0.01538848876953125, 0.01082611083984375, -0.008056640625, -0.00556182861328125, 0.0191497802734375, 0.0513916015625, 0.04107666015625, 0.036163330078125, 0.016632080078125, 0.0233917236328125, -0.01064300537109375, -0.038177490234375, 0.0151214599609375, 0.0140380859375, 0.0026721954345703125, -0.0251312255859375, -0.0005488395690917969, 0.0203704833984375, -0.00878143310546875, -0.0277862548828125, -0.006134033203125, -0.0092010498046875, 0.0140380859375, -0.018157958984375, -0.0187225341796875, -0.0155487060546875, -0.0026836395263671875, 0.0025157928466796875, -0.023956298828125, 0.0188140869140625, -0.0121917724609375, 0.032012939453125, 0.0153045654296875, -0.051422119140625, -0.0273590087890625, -0.0014600753784179688, 0.0169830322265625, -0.0295562744140625, 0.002361297607421875, 0.0233001708984375, -0.00978851318359375, 0.004791259765625, -0.0181732177734375, -0.004669189453125, 0.045074462890625, -0.01085662841796875, 0.01617431640625, 0.00212860107421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits [1\u20133]. A central challenge in this area is the transformation of the ordinary differential equations that govern a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process fundamental to system analysis and control design. While previous works have extended the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras, such as quaternions [4] and Clifford algebras [5], a comprehensive definition within the framework of Geometric Algebra remains lacking. This gap limits the ability to fully leverage GA's expressive power in the analysis of dynamical systems, and our work aims to address this specific shortcoming.\n\nIn this work, we introduce a definition of the Laplace transform specifically tailored for Geometric Algebras. Our approach generalizes existing definitions for hypercomplex algebras and provides a consistent framework for applying Laplace-domain techniques within GA. The definition is constructed by extending the classical Laplace transform to multivector-valued functions, treating each grade component separately and ensuring compatibility with the algebraic structure of GA. This allows for the transformation of signals and system equations where the variables and parameters are multivectors, not just scalars or vectors.\n\nWe focus on geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5. This restriction is motivated by two factors: first, algebras of this size are computationally tractable, as the dimension of the algebra grows exponentially with p + q, making larger signatures impractical for most analytical and numerical methods; second, signatures in this range encompass the most commonly used algebras in physics and engineering, such as the spacetime algebra (p, q) = (1,3) and the Euclidean algebra (p, q) = (3,0), ensuring practical relevance.\n\nWe present the formal definition of the Laplace transform in GA and establish its key properties, including linearity, the shifting theorem, and the treatment of multivector-valued functions. In particular, we address the challenge that arises when transforming multivector-valued functions: the Laplace transform must respect the multigrade structure and the non-commutative nature of the geometric product. Our definition ensures that each component is transformed consistently, preserving the relationships between grades.\n\nAs a main result, we demonstrate that our definition preserves the essential features of the classical Laplace transform while enabling its application to multivector-valued signals and systems. However, we note that our approach may be limited in cases where the algebra's dimension is very high (p + q > 5), due to computational complexity, and that certain pathological functions may not admit a Laplace transform in the GA setting, as in the classical case.\n\nThis advancement enables, for example, the analysis of RLC circuits where voltages and currents are naturally represented as multivectors, allowing for a unified treatment of scalar and vector quantities in the Laplace domain. The remainder of the paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews relevant background and related work; Section 3 introduces the formal definition and main properties of the GA Laplace transform; Section 4 discusses applications and limitations; and Section 5 concludes with directions for future research.\n\nReferences:\n[1] Doran, C., & Lasenby, A. (2003). Geometric Algebra for Physicists. Cambridge University Press.\n[2] Hitzer, E. (2013). Multivector Differential Calculus. Advances in Applied Clifford Algebras, 23(1), 181\u2013195.\n[3] Sangwine, S. J., & Ell, T. A. (2000). Hypercomplex Fourier Transforms of Color Images. IEEE Transactions on Image Processing, 16(1), 22\u201335.\n[4] Ell, T. A., & Sangwine, S. J. (2007). Quaternion Fourier Transforms for Signal and Image Processing. Advances in Imaging and Electron Physics, 140, 1\u201356.\n[5] Brackx, F., Delanghe, R., & Sommen, F. (1982). Clifford Analysis. Research Notes in Mathematics, 76."}}